# Claude Code Rules for Rust OSM Renderer

These rules enforce performance testing conventions for this project.

## Performance Testing Requirements

### When to Run Benchmarks

Run `cargo bench --bench tile_rendering` after changes to:
- `src/renderer/` - Any rendering code
- `src/data/mmap.rs` - Memory-mapped data access
- `src/data/serialization.rs` - Data serialization format
- `src/style/` - MapCSS evaluation
- `shaders/` - Shader code
- Any code in the hot path of tile rendering

### Performance Documentation

**REQUIRED**: When making performance-critical changes:

1. Run benchmarks before and after changes
2. Update `PERFORMANCE.md` with:
   - What changed
   - Performance impact (before/after timings)
   - Which tiles were tested
   - Whether <50ms target is maintained

3. Include performance results in commit message:
   ```
   Example commit message format:

   Optimize tag reading from memory-mapped data

   Performance impact:
   - Tile 11/1080/661: 2.7s → 15.7ms (172x speedup)
   - All tiles now <50ms target

   Changed memory-mapped view to read tags directly instead
   of opening file per object (eliminated 182K file opens).
   ```

### Performance Target

**STRICT REQUIREMENT**: All tiles MUST render in <50ms

- If any tile exceeds 50ms, the change must be optimized or reverted
- Document any tiles approaching the limit (>40ms) as potential concerns
- Maintain at least 20% headroom when possible

### Commit Requirements for Performance Changes

Every commit touching performance-critical files must:
- [ ] Include benchmark results in commit message
- [ ] Update PERFORMANCE.md if results changed significantly (>10%)
- [ ] Verify all test tiles still meet <50ms target
- [ ] Document any new performance optimizations applied

### Test Tiles

Standard performance test tiles:
- `11/1082/661` - Overflow edge case (430 objects)
- `11/1080/661` - High complexity (182K objects)

Add additional tiles to benchmarks if they reveal performance issues.

## Code Conventions

### Avoid Performance Anti-patterns

- ❌ Opening files in hot loops
- ❌ Allocating in per-object rendering code
- ❌ Unnecessary string cloning
- ❌ Repeated evaluation of same selectors

✅ Use memory-mapped data
✅ Pre-allocate buffers
✅ Cache evaluated styles
✅ Profile before optimizing

## Claude AI Assistant Behavior

When I (Claude Code) make changes to performance-critical code:

1. I MUST proactively run benchmarks after the change
2. I MUST ask if performance testing was done before committing
3. I MUST remind about updating PERFORMANCE.md
4. I MUST include performance impact in commit messages
5. I MUST flag if changes might affect the <50ms target
6. I MUST refuse to commit performance-critical changes without benchmark results

